Singly Linked List and Stack Implementation in C++

This code consists of two separate programs written in C++. The first program implements a singly linked list from scratch to simulate basic dynamic data structure operations. The second program demonstrates the use of C++ Standard Template Library stack to handle last-in-first-out data operations.

Overview of Program 1: Linked List
The linked list implementation defines a custom node structure containing an integer data value and a pointer to the next node.

The insert function adds new nodes at the head of the list. When inserting 10, then 20, then 30, each new element becomes the new head. This results in a reverse insertion order where the list becomes 30 -> 20 -> 10.

The remove function deletes the head node of the list, adjusts the head pointer to the next node, and frees the allocated memory to prevent memory leaks.

The Isempty function checks if the head pointer is set to null, returning true if the list contains no elements.

The display function traverses the linked list from head to tail using a temporary pointer and prints each element's data value separated by a space.

In the main function, values 10, 20, and 30 are inserted. The list displays as 30 20 10. Next, the remove function deletes the top element 30. The remaining list displays as 20 10. Finally, the program checks if the list is empty and prints that the list is not empty.

Overview of Program 2: STL Stack
The stack implementation wraps the built-in std::stack container functions inside custom utility functions.

The push function inserts an element onto the top of the stack.

The pop function checks whether the stack is empty. If not empty, it prints the top element and removes it from the stack.

The Isempty function checks whether the stack contains any elements using the empty member function.

In the main function, values 10, 20, and 30 are pushed onto the stack. Calling pop prints Popped: 30 and removes 30. Finally, the program checks if the stack is empty and prints that the stack is not empty.

Time Complexity Analysis

Program 1 Linked List
Insert operation: O(1) time complexity because nodes are inserted directly at the head.
Remove operation: O(1) time complexity because deletion happens directly at the head.
Isempty operation: O(1) time complexity because it performs a single pointer check against null.
Display operation: O(n) time complexity where n is the number of nodes, as it visits every node sequentially.
Overall space complexity: O(n) where n is the total number of dynamically allocated nodes.

Program 2 Stack
Push operation: O(1) time complexity.
Pop operation: O(1) time complexity.
Isempty operation: O(1) time complexity.
Overall space complexity: O(n) where n is the number of elements held inside the stack.

Sample Output

Program 1 Output:
30 20 10
20 10
List is not empty

Program 2 Output:
Popped: 30
Stack is not empty
